*Context: DeepSeek shocked the world last January, giving hope to Chinese
frontier model start-ups. In the year since, three Chinese tech giants have
struck back. This longform, deeply-sourced LatePost [=E6=99=9A=E7=82=B9] ar=
ticle chronicles
the three-way contest among ByteDance, Tencent, and Alibaba to build the AI
Super-App. *Below are notes on key passages, grouped by themes of
macro-level strategy, talent acquisition, and comparative advantages of the
giants vs. start-ups. The full translation is a 8,500-word, 20-pg. Google
Doc.

*Key Passages: When it comes to China=E2=80=99s AI super-app, everybody is =
chasing
ByteDance=E2=80=99s Doubao. Moonshot AI=E2=80=99s Kimi made waves with its =
long-prompt
processing capabilities in 2024; in early 2025, of course, DeepSeek=E2=80=
=99s
downloads briefly topped the charts; and Alibaba=E2=80=99s Qwen relaunched
successfully at the end of the year. Yet, as Honghao Gao reports, =E2=80=9C=
 Doubao
consistently remained in first place.=E2=80=9D*

   -

   Last year, ByteDance=E2=80=99s Doubao became the first AI product in Chi=
na to
   surpass 100 million daily active users. Doubao=E2=80=99s large model att=
ained an
   average daily token processing volume of 63 trillion, a 200% increase ov=
er
   the past six months.
   -

   In many ways, ByteDance was a sleeping giant. By the time ChatGPT
   launched in November 2022, Baidu, Huawei, and Alibaba had already releas=
ed
   their own LLMs, but ByteDance had not. The next year, ByteDance=E2=80=99=
s AI
   efforts stalled. Their AI creation platform Jimeng did not =E2=80=9Cbeco=
me the
   Douyin of the AI era=E2=80=9D, as they hoped.
   -

   In fact, the breakthrough was an AI assistant named Doubao, which
   benefited from ByteDance=E2=80=99s Douyin (shortform video platform) and=
 its unique
   voice call function. Regarding the former, videos of users =E2=80=9Ccrea=
tively
   training=E2=80=9D Doubao=E2=80=99s voice went viral on Douyin, which boa=
sts 800 million
   daily active users. As for the voice call function, the Doubao team:

=E2=80=9C*traveled across the country to collect dialects, refining the gra=
nularity
of accents down to the district and county level within cities=E2=80=A6and =
they
conducted a series of stylized dialogue training sessions on the model,
giving it a distinct personality*.=E2=80=9D


   -

   ByteDance also invested in its foundation model capabilities. In early
   February 2025, it hired Yonghui Wu, former Vice President of Google
   DeepMind, to manage theoretical research on large language models in its
   Seed team.

*Tencent goes all-in to catchup and leapfrog ByteDance. In 2025, it poured
more resources into its Yuanbao chatbot, which is based on its Hunyuan
large model. The clearest evidence of this shift came in September 2025,
when it poached 27-year-old Shunyu Yao, a former OpenAI researcher.*

   -

   First, let=E2=80=99s back up. Since 2023, Tencent took a cautious approa=
ch to
   LLMs, neither actively stockpiling compute nor making huge talent
   acquisitions. Its Yuanbao launched a year later than Doubao, and even th=
en,
   it was mostly positioned as =E2=80=9Ca product for Tencent to test its H=
unyuan
   large language model technology.=E2=80=9D An early Yuanbao team member t=
old
   LatePost, =E2=80=9C*Yuanbao was =E2=80=98in a state of begging other int=
ernal
   departments for collaboration*.=E2=80=99=E2=80=9D
   -

   After Yao took charge of Tencent=E2=80=99s large model, he called an int=
ernal
   meeting in November 2025, where he showed up wearing shorts and slippers=
.
   Gao reports, =E2=80=98*There are big problems with Hunyuan's evaluation,=
=E2=80=99 a
   person present at the meeting quoted Yao as saying, meaning that the mod=
el
   was overly focused on achieving high rankings on leaderboards,
   incorporating benchmark data into the training set, leading to data
   contamination. Although the model was good at answering questions, its
   performance in real-world scenarios was unstable. He hoped that, in the
   future, the team wouldn't focus on rankings or let the rankings dictate
   their work*.=E2=80=9C
   -

   Some of the fixes were unsexy affairs, such as addressing data
   standardization issues. Here=E2=80=99s the relevant LatePost passage: =
=E2=80=9C*A source
   close to Yuanbao told us that TEG=E2=80=99s data cleaning and labeling i=
n small
   model training were not standardized. Taking the LBS (location-based
   services) intent recognition model as an example: a user asking =E2=80=
=98What are
   some good restaurants nearby? [=E5=91=A8=E8=BE=B9=E6=9C=89=E4=BB=80=E4=
=B9=88=E5=A5=BD=E5=90=83=E7=9A=84=EF=BC=9F]=E2=80=99 is a (prompt with) cle=
ar intent;
   while =E2=80=98What would you recommend that are good things to eat [=E6=
=9C=89=E4=BB=80=E4=B9=88=E5=A5=BD=E5=90=83=E7=9A=84=E6=8E=A8=E8=8D=90]?=E2=
=80=99 is
   a (prompt with) vague intent. If these two types of samples were mixed a=
nd
   labeled together in the training set, the model would easily =E2=80=98le=
arn
   incorrectly,=E2=80=99 leading to an increased error rate.=E2=80=9D*
   -

   In addition to improving its Hunyuan model, Yuanbao also integrated
   DeepSeek. Tencent finally put in a big order on computing cards to deplo=
y
   DeepSeek across Tencent products, and Yuanbao climbed into the top three
